EXP[51] = [
 ('Reading the Genome in the Clinic', [
  "The first human genome cost about 2.7 billion dollars and took thirteen years. A genome can now be sequenced for a few hundred dollars in a day or two, and that change in price is what moved genetic information from research papers into ordinary appointments. The best-known uses are in families where a harmful variant runs through the generations. A woman who carries a harmful change in the *BRCA1* gene faces a lifetime risk of breast cancer somewhere between 55 and 72 percent, against about 12 percent for the general population, so knowing her status lets her choose closer screening or preventive surgery. Lynch syndrome, a hereditary risk of bowel and other cancers, works the same way: the test does not cure anything, but it tells the family where to look.",
  "The most striking successes are drugs built for one genetic cause. Imatinib, approved in 2001, blocks the abnormal protein made by the fusion gene that drives chronic myeloid leukemia. Before it, only about a third of patients were alive five years after diagnosis, and afterward roughly nine in ten were. In cystic fibrosis, a single faulty protein can now be helped by a combination of drugs that nudge the damaged protein into working form, and the most recent combination suits about nine in ten patients. In each case the path ran from a cause found in the genome, to a molecule that fits it, to a trial in the patients who carry it.",
 ]),
 ('Why a Risk Score Is Not a Verdict', [
  "A polygenic risk score adds up the small effects of thousands of common variants into a single number. For a trait like height, such scores now predict a substantial share of the differences between people in the populations they were built from. For common diseases the predictions are weaker, and they tell you where a person sits in a distribution, not what will happen to them. Two people in the top tenth of a heart-disease score will not both fall ill; many in the bottom tenth will.",
  "There is also a fairness problem that scientists have openly acknowledged. Most large genetic studies have drawn on people of European ancestry, so scores built from them perform markedly worse in people of African or Asian ancestry, whose genetic variation is different. A score that works well for one group and poorly for another can widen health gaps instead of closing them. The remedy is plain and slow: study many more people from many more populations, and say honestly where a tool has been tested and where it has not.",
  "A score earns a place in a clinic only if it changes what a doctor does, and does so in a way that helps. A high cholesterol result already tells a doctor to consider a statin, so a genetic score adds little for the patient whose numbers are clearly high. The score may matter most for the person whose conventional numbers look normal but whose family history is not, and trials are now asking whether acting on it saves lives.",
 ]),
 ('Therapies Written for One Gene', [
  "Gene therapy delivers a working copy of a gene into a patient’s cells, usually inside a harmless virus. The first treatments targeted rare inherited diseases. One of them, for a form of spinal muscular atrophy that once killed most affected infants, is a single infusion that can largely prevent the disease. The prices of these treatments run to millions of dollars per patient, and the figure is a fair summary of the field’s problem: the science can work, while the number of people who can be treated depends on manufacturing, safety monitoring, and who pays.",
  "Gene editing adds a second route. In 2023 regulators approved the first therapy based on CRISPR editing, for sickle cell disease and beta-thalassemia. Cells are taken from the patient, edited to switch on fetal hemoglobin, and returned after the patient’s own marrow has been cleared. The results in trials were striking and the procedure is arduous and costly. Treatments that are simpler, cheaper, and given in a single injection are the aim of the next generation. Chapter 42 describes the tools behind them.",
 ]),
]

EXP[52] = [
 ('The Hallmarks of Aging', [
  "Researchers who wanted to bring order to the field proposed in 2013 a list of nine hallmarks, features that appear in aging tissues, can be made worse in experiments, and can be improved in animals when treated. The list grew to twelve in 2023. It includes damage to the genome, shortening of the protective ends of chromosomes, changes in the chemical marks that regulate genes, and the loss of the cell’s ability to fold and clear proteins. It also includes disturbed nutrient sensing, failing mitochondria, cells that stop dividing yet linger and signal damage, exhaustion of stem cells, and altered communication between cells. The newer additions are failing cellular housekeeping, chronic low-grade inflammation, and a disturbed community of gut microbes.",
  "The value of the list is that it turns a vague process into measurable parts. Its weakness is that the hallmarks interact, and nobody yet knows which are causes and which are consequences. A treatment that fixes one may leave the others unchanged, or worse, trade one for another. The wide agreement that these are the right things to study is real progress; the agreement that any one of them is the master switch does not exist.",
 ]),
 ('Counting Divisions: Telomeres', [
  "In 1961 Leonard Hayflick found that normal human cells grown in a dish divide only about fifty times and then stop. The counting mechanism is largely the telomere, a repeating stretch of DNA at the end of each chromosome that works like the plastic tip on a shoelace. Each division shortens it by tens of bases, because the copying machinery cannot quite reach the very end. When the telomeres become critically short, the cell stops dividing or dies.",
  "An enzyme called telomerase can rebuild the ends, and Elizabeth Blackburn, Carol Greider, and Jack Szostak shared the 2009 Nobel Prize for working it out. It is active in embryonic cells and in some stem cells, and silent in most adult tissue. It is switched back on in roughly nine of ten cancers, which is a clear warning about turning it on casually. The cell’s ability to divide without limit is the property cancer needs, and the limit is one of the body’s defenses.",
 ]),
 ('Why Evolution Allows Aging', [
  "If aging is harmful, why has it not been selected away? Peter Medawar gave the first answer in 1952. In nature almost every animal is eventually eaten, starved, or killed by disease, so very few survive to be old. A gene that does damage only late in life is therefore almost invisible to natural selection, because few carriers live long enough for it to matter. George Williams added in 1957 that a gene can be selected despite late harm if it helps early, by improving growth or reproduction. Thomas Kirkwood’s disposable-soma idea adds a budget: energy spent on perfect repair is energy not spent on reproducing, and evolution tends to choose the cheaper compromise.",
  "The theory makes predictions that have been confirmed. Animals that face fewer predators, like bats, tortoises, and some birds, tend to live longer for their size. Guppies moved to streams with fewer predators evolved slower aging within about a dozen years. In humans, the risk of dying doubles roughly every eight years after early adulthood, a pattern called the Gompertz law. With a yearly risk of about 1 in 1,000 at age 30, that doubling puts the risk near 6 percent at 78 and a quarter at 94. Any real anti-aging treatment would need to bend that curve, not merely shift where we are on it.",
 ]),
]

EXP[66] = [
 ('Sensing, Thinking, Acting', [
  "Every robot runs a loop. Sensors report the state of the world, software decides what to do, and motors act, and then the loop repeats many times a second. The oldest and still most common control rule is feedback: measure the error between where you are and where you want to be, and push in proportion to it. A thermostat does this slowly; a robot arm does it a thousand times a second, adding terms that anticipate overshoot and correct for a steady drift. Most of the smoothness of industrial motion comes from this unglamorous mathematics.",
  "Sensors are never exact, so robots combine several. A wheel encoder counts rotations but slips on dust. A gyroscope gives rotation rate but drifts. A camera sees landmarks but is fooled by shadows. A filter, of which the Kalman filter is the classic example, weighs each source by how far it can be trusted and produces a better estimate than any one alone. The same idea guided the Apollo spacecraft to the Moon. It is why a self-driving car can track a pedestrian even when a lamp post briefly hides them.",
 ]),
 ('Why the Hand Is Hard', [
  "The roboticist Hans Moravec noticed in the 1980s that the things people find hard, like chess or algebra, were easier for machines than the things a toddler does effortlessly, like walking across a cluttered room or picking up a grape. Evolution spent hundreds of millions of years perfecting perception and movement and only the last instant on abstract reasoning, so the old skills are deeply optimized while the new ones are shallow and, as it turns out, easy to program.",
  "The human hand shows how deep the optimization runs. It has about twenty-seven degrees of freedom, many hundreds of touch receptors in each fingertip, and a nervous system that adjusts grip within a few hundredths of a second when an object slips. A typical industrial robot arm has six or seven joints and, until recently, almost no sense of touch. Engineers now build hands with many joints and tactile skins, and learning methods can improve their use. In 2019 researchers at OpenAI trained a robot hand, in simulation, to rotate a Rubik’s cube and then transferred the skill to a real hand. It worked, though it failed often on hard scrambles, and the gap between simulation and reality was exactly the difficulty that this chapter describes.",
 ]),
 ('Where Robots Already Work', [
  "Surgical systems controlled by a surgeon at a console have been in use since 2000 and have been used in millions of operations. They do not decide anything; they translate the surgeon’s hand movements into smaller, steadier ones and filter out tremor. Farm robots milk cows on their own schedule, and newer machines identify weeds with cameras and spray only them. Warehouses run fleets numbering in the hundreds of thousands of mobile robots. Mars has been explored by rovers that must plan their own route because a radio message takes minutes each way.",
  "The pattern across these examples is that robots succeed where someone has simplified the problem: a bounded task, a known environment, a human who handles the exceptions. After the 2011 accident at Fukushima, robots sent into the damaged reactors struggled with rubble and stairs, and radiation disabled their electronics. The lesson drawn then still holds: in the places where we most want robots, the world is least prepared for them.",
 ]),
]

EXP[67] = [
 ('From Gene to Tank', [
  "A modern biological drug begins as a gene, which is built chemically, inserted into a host cell, and checked. Bacteria such as *E. coli* are cheap and fast and are used for simpler proteins like insulin. Yeast can add some of the sugar decorations that larger proteins need. Complex proteins, including most antibodies, are made in cultured mammalian cells, usually from Chinese hamster ovary lines that have been used for decades. The cells grow in stainless steel or single-use bioreactors, fed a measured broth, and held at a constant temperature, acidity, and oxygen level.",
  "The numbers show why this is an industry. A good antibody process yields several grams of product per liter of culture. A bioreactor of 10,000 liters at 5 grams per liter therefore delivers about 50 kilograms in a run. At a dose of 200 milligrams, that is about 250,000 doses from one tank. Purification takes most of the effort and cost: the product has to be separated from the host’s own proteins and DNA, and tested to show that every batch matches the last. The cost of a biologic is largely the cost of that sameness.",
 ]),
 ('Crops and Food', [
  "Genetic engineering of crops began in the 1980s and 1990s. Insect-resistant crops carry a gene from a soil bacterium that produces a protein toxic to certain caterpillars and harmless to people, which reduces the need to spray insecticides. Papaya in Hawaii was rescued in the 1990s by a variety engineered to resist the ringspot virus that had been destroying the harvest. A 2016 review by the US National Academies found no substantiated evidence that approved engineered crops carry greater risk to health than conventionally bred ones, while noting that each new product needs its own assessment, and that effects on farms and ecosystems depend on how a crop is used.",
  "Some of the oldest products of biotechnology are in the grocery store without being noticed. Much of the cheese in the United States is made with chymosin, the milk-curdling enzyme, produced by engineered microbes since 1990 rather than extracted from calf stomachs. Newer gene-editing techniques make small, precise changes, such as a mushroom that does not brown or a tomato with more of a nutrient, and regulators in different countries are still deciding whether to treat them like conventional breeding. The scientific question, what exactly changed in the plant, has an answer. The legal and cultural question, how that change should be labeled, has several.",
 ]),
 ('Enzymes, Fuels and Industrial Chemistry', [
  "Laundry detergents contain enzymes that break down proteins, starches, and fats, and have for decades, letting clothes be cleaned in cooler water. Bread, beer, and cheese depend on microbes, and so do many vitamins and flavors. Industrial biology extends the older craft by redesigning the enzymes themselves; in 2018 Frances Arnold shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for directed evolution, a method that mutates an enzyme at random and keeps the variant that works better, repeating the cycle until the enzyme does something new.",
  "The arguments over fuels are a lesson in accounting. Ethanol from corn is a mature product, but it uses more than a third of the US corn harvest, and the net greenhouse gas saving compared with petrol is modest and disputed. Sugar cane in Brazil gives a better balance. Artemisinin, the most effective antimalarial drug, was long extracted from sweet wormwood plants whose supply swung with farm prices; a yeast engineered by Jay Keasling’s group made the precursor chemical in tanks, and commercial production began in 2013. Plant prices then fell, and the engineered route struggled to compete. Biology can make a molecule. Whether it makes it cheaply enough depends on the price of the alternative.",
 ]),
]
